Z||000000||swoods||07-31-2001||02:07 PM|||| Alright I have a 1984 2.5 fiero and I want to get the most out of my car that is possible, I don't want to spend a fortune on it neither and I want the car as reliable as possible. I would appreciate everyone's help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||saleenfiero||07-31-2001||02:54 PM||||Welcome.  This is possibly the most dicussed topic on this forum.  So instead of repeating what has been said 4 billion times, just use the search engine on the forum and use keywords which apply to you.  You'll get more info than you'll ever want.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HummelHund||07-31-2001||03:19 PM||||Welcome to the forum and to Fiero ownership! I too have an 84' Fiero, an SE, as well as an 84' Sport Coupe. Though I've done alot of repair work to my car (it was sitting in a field for a couple of years), the best thing I ever did for that car was a full tune-up/oil change. I went to my local AutoZone, and bought: distributor cap and rotor, plugs and plug wires, two new belts (A/C and main), a fuel filter, an oil filter and oil, a new breather filter (don't get deutsch, they don't fit right), a new air filter, a new thermostat, and a new pcv valve. It cost me about $80 but was money well spent. I would also recommend replacing all the vacuum hoses, as they are one of the main causes of 2.5L Fiero problems. Also inspect the coolant hoses and replace them if they're looking old. If you're feeling adventurous and want to spend a little more a new EGR valve would not be a waste, just make sure to get an AC Delco one. None of this stuff will cost very much and will go a long way to ensuring that your Fiero will be as reliable as possible. You'll probably have to replace some sensors further on down the line, but none of them are very expensive. <BR>Good luck with your Fiero!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe, The "Black Wedge of Death" ($150 running car)||||reg||1
